values
  ('df2136ac-9fe4-5ea0-8a0c-efc12373bb1d', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-word-parts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Both "eruption" and "disrupt" contain the root rupt, meaning "break." The prefix e- means "out," and dis- means "apart." Which statement best contrasts the two words as they are used in the passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both words mean that the ground slowly swells, like a balloon being filled with air.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-root-clues"},{"text":"An eruption is magma breaking out, while to disrupt is to break apart people's usual routines.","is_correct":true},{"text":"An eruption breaks apart people's routines, while to disrupt is magma breaking out of the ground.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-prefix-meanings"},{"text":"An eruption is a small earthquake, while to disrupt is to record shaking with a pen.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-related-details"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$An eruption is magma breaking out, while to disrupt is to break apart people's usual routines.$q$, $q$The prefixes change how the "break" happens: magma breaks out in an eruption, and ash breaks apart daily life when it disrupts flights and roads. The choice saying an eruption "breaks apart people's routines" uses the right ideas but attaches each one to the wrong word.$q$, 'draft', 'bb8241f0-37f6-5ff9-8156-bf2bbffe9f5a')
