-- Passage rg7-word-parts-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fc1b71d7-429d-5bf8-8214-cd5d24f0039d', 'rg7-word-parts-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Captain's Plan$q$, $q$With ten minutes left in the championship game, the Eastside Falcons were tied with the Harbor City Hawks. Rain had started to fall, and the field was slick. Coach Bellamy called a timeout and waved her players to the sideline.

The team's captain, Priya Raman, was usually quiet. Today she projected her voice so that even the players at the far end of the huddle could hear her. "Their defense keeps shifting left," she said. "If we pass right early, we'll have space."

A defender named Jonah interjected before she could finish. "But they've blocked every pass on that side," he said.

Priya did not want to contradict her coach's earlier plan, so she looked at Coach Bellamy. The coach nodded slowly. "Jonah's right that they've blocked it before," she said. "But they've been tired since halftime. Their movements have become predictable. Let's try Priya's idea."

Two players argued for a different play, but the coach rejected it. There was no time to try three ideas, she explained. One clear plan was better than three half plans.

The whistle blew. Within a minute, Priya sent a long pass to the right side, and Marisol Vega raced onto it. Her shot curled past the goalkeeper and into the net. When the final whistle sounded, the Falcons had won by one goal. On the bus ride home, Jonah admitted that the captain's plan had worked.$q$, 'draft')
