   $q$The passage uses both "telegraph" and "photograph." The root graph means "write or draw," and photo means "light." Which statement best explains the root graph in each word?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both words name a chart with lines and numbers, so graph means \"chart\" in each one.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"Both words mean sending something far away, so graph must mean \"far\" in each one.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-roots"},{"text":"A telegraph speaks a message out loud, and a photograph speaks with bright light.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-root-meaning"},{"text":"A telegraph writes a message from far away, and a photograph is a picture drawn with light.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A telegraph writes a message from far away, and a photograph is a picture drawn with light.$q$, $q$Graph keeps its meaning of "write or draw" in both words, while the other root changes what is written: messages from far away, or images made by light. The choice claiming that graph must mean "far" gives graph the meaning of tele, which only appears in telegraph.$q$, 'draft', '8b40a0a8-dbf0-598f-8321-e207b978a0c5')
