-- Passage rg7-word-parts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f900845c-2d04-5c22-b373-d9d6cbcf6f9c', 'rg7-word-parts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Beaver's Building Project$q$, $q$Few animals change their surroundings as much as the beaver. Using only their teeth and paws, beavers construct dams across streams. A dam slows the water and creates a pond, which gives the beavers a safe place to live.

The dam is made of branches, mud, and stones. Beavers drag logs into place and pack mud into the gaps. If a section begins to leak, they hurry to repair it, often working through the night. Some dams last for many years because each new generation keeps fixing them.

In the middle of the pond, beavers build a home called a lodge. From the outside, the lodge looks like a messy pile of sticks. Inside, however, its structure is carefully planned. The entrances are submerged below the water's surface, so predators cannot easily follow the beavers inside. Above the water, a dry room holds the family.

Biologists who study beavers have found that their ponds help many other living things. Ducks, frogs, and fish move into the calm water. Plants grow along the new shoreline. Even creatures too tiny to see without a microscope thrive in the still pond.

Not everyone welcomes beavers, though. Their dams can flood roads and farm fields. Some towns now install pipes through the dams. These pipes let extra water drain away while leaving the structure in place. In this way, people and beavers can share the same stream.$q$, 'draft')
