-- Passage rg7-word-parts-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('edb3f6fa-e5cf-5542-ad86-5cf94ab240ca', 'rg7-word-parts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Mural on Fifth Street$q$, $q$For most of the summer, the brick wall beside the hardware store on Fifth Street was blank and gray. Then the town's art council chose Keiko Tanaka, a high school senior, to paint a mural there. Her design would honor Eleanor Briggs, a nurse who had opened the town's first free clinic eighty years earlier.

Keiko started by reading a biography of Ms. Briggs at the public library. The book described how the nurse had walked from farm to farm with a medical bag. Keiko filled a sketchbook with ideas based on those stories.

The hardest part was perspective. Keiko wanted the road in the painting to look as if it stretched far into the distance. She practiced drawing lines that came together at a single point, until the road seemed to disappear over a hill.

Volunteers helped with the background colors. A retired sign painter named Mr. Abernathy offered to paint an inscription along the bottom of the wall. In neat white letters, it read: "She walked so others could heal."

When the mural was finished, Keiko stepped across the street to look. From there, every detail was visible, even the tiny bandage on the nurse's hand. A small crowd gathered. An older woman told Keiko that her own mother had been one of Ms. Briggs's patients.

"I didn't know that," Keiko said quietly. She realized the mural was not only about the past. It belonged to the people who still remembered it.$q$, 'draft')
