-- Passage rg8-argument-evidence-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7849adca-914b-5525-9f9e-efb71da06e23', 'rg8-argument-evidence-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Keep the Library Open Later$q$, $q$Every afternoon at 3:15, the doors of the Halvorsen Middle School library lock, and dozens of students lose their best place to study. The school should keep the library open until five o'clock on weekdays.

First, many students have nowhere quiet to work after school. In a survey of 210 Halvorsen students last spring, 64 percent said their homes are noisy in the afternoon. Another 41 percent said they do not have a reliable internet connection at home. The library offers quiet tables, computers and free internet.

Second, longer hours would help students keep up with their classes. Corbin Middle School extended its library hours two years ago. In the first year, the number of late homework assignments there dropped by nearly a third. Teachers at Corbin also reported that more students finished their projects on time.

Third, the cost would be small. One adult and a team of trained student volunteers could staff the library. The school already pays to heat and light the building until six o'clock because of sports practices.

The library also has a cheerful reading corner with beanbag chairs, which the art club painted last year. Visitors often say it is the friendliest room in the building.

Some people argue that students should go home and rest after a long school day. Rest matters, but no student would be required to stay. The library would simply be there for the students who need it.

Keeping the library open two more hours is a simple change. It would give every student a fair chance to do their best work.$q$, 'draft')
