-- Passage rg8-argument-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('891e9a60-08d6-552e-9a3b-cd23a10a8534', 'rg8-argument-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Smaller Fields for Younger Players$q$, $q$In the Brookhaven Youth Soccer League, eight-year-olds play eleven against eleven on the same full-size field used by high school teams. The league should switch its under-ten teams to seven players a side on a smaller field.

The main reason is touches on the ball. Young players improve by handling the ball, not by watching others handle it. Last fall, the league's coaches filmed twelve practice games and counted every touch. On a full field, each player touched the ball about 18 times per game. In seven-a-side games on a small field, each player averaged 41 touches.

Smaller games also mean more chances to make decisions. With fewer teammates, each player has to pass, dribble and defend instead of waiting at the edge of the play. Goalkeepers face more shots and get more practice too.

The league's new uniforms arrived this year, and the blue and gold colors look sharp on every team.

My cousin Tomas played on small fields until he was eleven, and now he is the best player on his high school team. That shows how well small fields work.

Small fields will also make players enjoy the game more, so fewer of them will quit.

Some parents say young players should learn on a real field from the start. But a smaller field is still real soccer. Players simply move up to the full field when their skills and bodies are ready for it.

The league can make this change before next season with nothing more than paint and smaller goals.$q$, 'draft')
