insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1320f481-cf9c-5b60-9035-011cd5957c22', 'rg8-argument-evidence-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Streetlight Debate of 1889$q$, $q$In the spring of 1889, the town council of Millbrook met to decide whether to replace its gas lamps with electric streetlights. The debate filled the town hall for two nights, and the arguments on each side were recorded in the council's minutes.

Council member Augusta Fenwick spoke in favor of the lights. She presented records from the nearby town of Dalton, which had installed electric lights a year earlier. Dalton's records showed that wagon accidents after dark fell from 23 to 9 in the first year. Fenwick also reported that six Main Street shop owners had promised to stay open two hours later if the street were lit.

Council member Thaddeus Moreau spoke against the plan. He pointed out that the Millbrook Electric Company had been formed only four months earlier and had never built a lighting system anywhere. If the company failed, the town could be left with broken lights and no one to repair them.

Moreau made other arguments as well. He claimed that bright electric light would harm the eyes of horses, though he offered no reports or examples. He also reminded the council that Fenwick had lived in Millbrook for only five years.

After the second night, the council reached a compromise. It voted to light Main Street first as a test. The company would light the rest of the town only if the test lights worked for a full year.$q$, 'draft')
