insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1234fd4d-f2ca-5e66-9c39-f11c37341b24', 'rg8-argument-evidence-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Keep the Summer Mural Program$q$, $q$For six summers, the city of Ridgeview has paid teenage artists to paint murals on blank public walls. This year, the city council is considering ending the program to save money. The council should keep it.

The murals protect walls from graffiti. City maintenance records show that the nine walls now covered by murals received 40 graffiti reports in the year before they were painted. In the year after, those same walls received only 6 reports. Painting over graffiti costs the city time and money, so fewer reports also mean lower cleaning costs.

The program also gives teenagers paid work and real training. Over six summers, 42 teens have been hired. Each one learned to plan a design, measure a wall and work with a team on a deadline.

Local businesses benefit as well. Last fall, a student volunteer asked 15 shop owners near the murals whether they had noticed a change. Eleven said they believed more people were walking past their stores.

People have painted on walls for thousands of years, from cave paintings to the frescoes of old palaces.

Some council members argue that the money should go to road repair instead. Roads matter, but the mural program costs less than one percent of the city's yearly maintenance budget. Ending it would barely change the condition of the roads.

The murals make Ridgeview brighter, cleaner and more welcoming. The council should vote to keep them coming.$q$, 'draft')
