-- Passage rg8-argument-evidence-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ecc248a0-2807-54eb-b802-b752f8eaa63d', 'rg8-argument-evidence-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why Volunteer Bird Counts Matter$q$, $q$Each winter, thousands of volunteers walk through parks, fields and backyards to count birds. Some scientists once doubted that counts made by volunteers could be trusted. Today, volunteer bird counts are a valuable source of scientific data.

First, the counts are organized carefully. Volunteers follow set routes and count for a set amount of time. New counters attend a training session and often walk with an experienced partner before counting alone.

Second, the data are checked. When a volunteer reports a bird that is rare for the area, a regional reviewer contacts the volunteer. The reviewer asks for a photo or a detailed description before the report is accepted.

Third, volunteer counts have been tested against expert counts. In one study, trained biologists counted birds at 30 of the same sites that volunteers counted on the same days. The two sets of totals matched within 10 percent at 27 of the sites.

Birdwatching is also a relaxing hobby that many people enjoy on weekends.

Critics point out that volunteers sometimes misidentify birds. That is true, but a few mistakes have little effect on totals gathered from thousands of counters. The review system also catches many errors before they enter the records.

Finally, volunteers make it possible to gather data across huge areas for many years. No team of paid scientists could cover so much ground every winter.

Volunteer data have proved reliable for tracking birds. In fact, volunteer data are just as good as expert data for every kind of science.$q$, 'draft')
