-- Passage rg8-authors-purpose-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b2b90ff1-915e-5d1d-9a20-f036f60d28c7', 'rg8-authors-purpose-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Leave the Leaves$q$, $q$Every fall, many homeowners rake their lawns until not a single leaf remains. They stuff the leaves into bags and leave them at the curb for the town to collect. I would like to suggest a different approach: leave at least some of the leaves where they fall.

Fallen leaves are not trash. They are part of a natural cycle. As leaves break down over the winter, they return nutrients to the soil, which helps grass, trees, and garden plants grow in spring. A layer of leaves also shelters many small creatures. Some caterpillars, beetles, and spiders spend the winter tucked beneath them, and the birds that return in spring depend on those insects for food.

Bagging leaves has costs as well. Trucks must haul them away, and in some towns the bags end up in landfills, where they take up space.

Many people object that a yard covered in leaves looks messy, and some neighborhood associations even require tidy lawns. Others point out, correctly, that a thick, wet mat of leaves can smother grass. These are reasonable points, and I am not suggesting that anyone let leaves pile a foot deep across the front lawn.

Instead, rake leaves off the grass and into garden beds or under shrubs, where they can do their work out of sight. Or run a mower over a thin layer to chop the leaves into small pieces that disappear into the lawn. With a little planning, a yard can look neat and still help the creatures that live there.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
