-- Passage rg8-comparing-texts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5c898882-23a6-5f13-b007-0f4e73a3f581', 'rg8-comparing-texts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Calling and Answering$q$, $q$Text 1: Why Firefly Carries a Light (a tale)

Long ago, Firefly was a plain brown beetle, and she was lonely. At night the meadow was so dark that she could never find her friends.

One evening, Firefly flew all the way to the Moon and asked for help. The Moon felt sorry for her. She broke off a tiny chip of her own glow and pressed it onto Firefly's back. "Use it to call to others," the Moon said, "and they will answer."

Firefly flew home and blinked her new light across the grass. Far away, another small light blinked back. Then another. Soon the meadow sparkled, and Firefly was never lonely again.

That is why fireflies blink to one another on summer nights. It is also why the Moon looks a little smaller on some nights than on others.

Text 2: Field Notes (a story)

Every July, Aiyana's grandfather told her the story of Firefly and the Moon. This year, Aiyana had a new reason to watch the meadow. Her science club was counting fireflies for a university project.

At dusk she sat in the grass with a clipboard and a timer. Her club leader had explained that each kind of firefly flashes in its own pattern. The flashes are signals. A firefly flashes, and another of its kind answers.

Aiyana counted twelve flashes in the first minute. Then she noticed something. When one light blinked near the fence, a second light answered from the tall grass, almost like a reply.

On the walk home, she told her grandfather what she had learned. He listened, then laughed. "So the Moon was right," he said. "They do call, and they do answer."$q$, 'draft')
