insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('35895a96-24f3-5dff-871e-de114a040774', 'rg8-comparing-texts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Music Close to Home$q$, $q$Text 1: The Farthest Song (a tale)

A young flute player named Bastien wanted to play the most beautiful song in the world. Nobody in his village could teach it to him, so he packed his flute and set out.

In the city of towers he heard musicians play for a king. Their music was grand, but it did not move him. In the desert he heard drummers play under the stars. Their music was strong, but it was not the song he wanted. He traveled for three years.

At last, tired and homesick, he returned to his village at dusk. The women were singing as they carried water from the well. The children were clapping a rhythm on the steps. An old man whistled from his doorway.

Bastien stood very still. Then he lifted his flute and began to play along.

Text 2: Sound Check (a story)

Nadia needed a soundtrack for her two-minute film about her neighborhood, and nothing felt right. She searched music sites for hours. She tried orchestras, electronic beats and a famous piano piece. Every track sounded like it belonged to some other place.

Two days before the deadline, she gave up and sat on her front steps with her phone. The bus brakes sighed at the corner. Mr. Haddad's radio played from his open window. The twins next door were jumping rope and chanting a counting rhyme.

Nadia pressed record.

That night she layered the sounds together: the brakes as a low note, the radio as a melody, the rope slapping the pavement as a beat. When her class watched the film, someone asked where she had found the music.

"Out front," Nadia said.$q$, 'draft')
