-- Passage rg8-comparing-texts-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ea3796cd-f7da-5600-9418-b6f0a7058cea', 'rg8-comparing-texts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Careful What You Ask For$q$, $q$Text 1: The Miller and the Wind (a tale)

Once there was a miller whose windmill turned only when the wind blew. On still days, the great sails hung limp, and he could grind no flour.

One calm morning, the miller climbed the hill and shouted to the sky. "Wind, blow forever, and never rest!"

The wind heard him. It blew that day and the next and the next. The mill sails spun so fast that they whistled. The miller ground more flour than he could sell.

But the wind did not stop. It tore the shutters off his house. It scattered his neighbors' hay across three valleys. It dried the river until the mill wheel downstream stood still.

On the seventh day, the miller climbed the hill again. "Wind," he said quietly, "blow when you must, and rest when you must." The air grew calm. The next morning, a gentle breeze returned.

Text 2: Focus Mode (a story)

Ezra had an essay due Friday and a phone that buzzed every few minutes. On Wednesday he downloaded an app that promised total focus. He set it to block every message, every call and every alert for six hours.

It worked. He wrote four pages without looking up once.

When he finished, he walked to the corner where his sister always picked him up after the library closed. She was not there. He waited twenty minutes, then forty. When he finally unlocked his phone, he found eleven messages. Her car had a flat tire. Could he take the bus? Was he okay? Their mom had called twice.

That night, Ezra opened the app again. He did not delete it. He changed one setting so that messages from his family would always come through.$q$, 'draft')
