-- Passage rg8-connections-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a50e6553-0877-5b33-a01d-efd83588728b', 'rg8-connections-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How a Food Bank Sorts Donations$q$, $q$Every week, the Eastside Community Food Bank receives about three tons of donated food. Before any of it reaches a family, volunteers sort it into three categories. The categories are not based on the type of food, such as fruit or grain. Instead, they are based on how long the food can safely be kept.

The first category is shelf-stable food. This includes canned soup, dry pasta, rice, and peanut butter in sealed jars. These items can sit on a pantry shelf for months, so volunteers stack them in the main warehouse and hand them out in order of their dates.

The second category is perishable food, such as fresh vegetables, bread, milk, and eggs. These items spoil within days, so they move straight to the cooler or onto the distribution tables. A head of lettuce and a carton of milk look nothing alike, but they belong in the same category because both are in a race against time.

The third category surprises many new volunteers. It is called "hold for review." Dented cans, packages with torn labels, and anything without a clear date go here. A trained staff member checks each item before deciding whether it is safe to give out.

Sorting by storage time instead of food type helps the food bank waste less. Under the old system, apples sat in the fruit section next to canned peaches until they turned soft. Now, fresh food leaves the building first, and canned food waits its turn.

Volunteer coordinator Marisol Etienne explains the system to new helpers with a simple question. She asks them to imagine where each item would be safest in their own kitchen.$q$, 'draft')
