-- Passage rg8-context-clues-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3cd9f915-b6b7-578f-bfa5-7dd26668d077', 'rg8-context-clues-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Running the Long Way$q$, $q$A marathon is 26.2 miles long, and finishing one is a test of endurance, the ability to keep going for a long time without stopping. Sprinters need bursts of speed, but marathon runners need bodies that can work steadily for three, four, or even five hours.

During a run, muscles burn a fuel called glycogen, which the body stores in the muscles and liver. The supply is limited. After about two hours of hard running, many runners deplete their glycogen, using up nearly all of what they had stored. When that happens, they may suddenly feel as if their legs are made of stone. Runners call this moment hitting the wall, because it feels like crashing into a barrier that will not let them move forward.

To avoid hitting the wall, experienced runners plan their speed carefully. Starting too fast is a common mistake, since early excitement makes it tempting to run faster than the body can sustain. Most coaches advise a steady speed that a runner can keep up for the whole race, rather than an exhausting sprint that cannot be maintained.

Training for a marathon is strenuous. Runners spend months building up their weekly mileage, and some of their practice runs last longer than a full movie. The effort leaves them tired and sore, which is why rest days are part of every good training plan.

Runners who race in a hot or high place must also adjust. By arriving a week or two early, they give their bodies time to get used to the thin mountain air or the thick summer heat.$q$, 'draft')
