insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('71b51725-6704-547c-8957-ff13f000b1a2', 'rg8-evidence-inference-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Keepers of the Light$q$, $q$Before electric lamps and automatic timers, every lighthouse needed a keeper. The keeper's main duty was to keep the lamp burning from sunset to sunrise. In the 1800s, many lamps burned whale oil and later kerosene. These fuels left black soot on the glass, so keepers cleaned the lens and polished the brass fittings nearly every morning.

The work did not stop when the sun came up. Keepers trimmed lamp wicks, carried heavy fuel cans up winding stairs, and painted the tower to protect it from salt air. Many also kept a daily logbook. In it they recorded the weather, passing ships, and any repairs they made. Inspectors visited without warning and checked these logbooks, along with the shine on every piece of metal.

Families often shared the work. A keeper's children might help carry supplies or watch the lamp during a long storm. At remote stations, the family's nearest neighbors could be many miles away by boat. Supply ships brought food and fuel only a few times each year, so families planted gardens and kept chickens when the rocky ground allowed it.

By the end of the twentieth century, most lighthouses had switched to electric lights that turned on by themselves. Keepers were no longer needed, and many stations were left empty. Today, some old logbooks sit in museums. Historians read them to learn about storms, shipwrecks, and daily life along the coast in earlier times.$q$, 'draft')
