-- Passage rg8-evidence-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c4cb6e84-5cb9-5608-acfd-145dea8d4e7f', 'rg8-evidence-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why Rest Days Matter$q$, $q$Many young athletes believe that the harder they train, the faster they will improve. Training does make muscles stronger, but not in the way most people think. During a hard workout, muscle fibers develop tiny tears. The body repairs those tears during rest, and the repaired fibers come back a little thicker and stronger. In other words, the improvement happens after the workout, not during it.

Skipping rest interrupts this repair. An athlete who trains hard every day may feel tired, sore, or slower instead of faster. Coaches call this overtraining. Its signs include trouble sleeping, a lack of interest in practice, and small injuries that keep coming back. Some athletes respond by training even harder, which only makes the problem worse.

Sleep is one of the most important parts of rest. While a person sleeps, the body releases substances that help rebuild muscle and bone. Teenagers generally need more sleep than adults, yet many get less because of early school start times, homework, and screens at night.

Rest does not always mean lying on the couch. Coaches often recommend "active recovery," such as an easy bike ride, a slow swim, or gentle stretching. These activities keep blood flowing to tired muscles without adding much strain.

A soccer coach named Dmitri Varga explains this to his team at the start of each season. He tells players that a rest day is part of the training plan, not a break from it. His team schedules at least one full rest day every week, even during tournaments.$q$, 'draft')
